{"question": "How should organizations validate system inputs to prevent injection or cross-site scripting attacks?", "framework_version": "rev5", "answer_text": "Organizations should check the syntax and semantics of system inputs, including character set, length, numerical range, and acceptable values. They should also use parameterized interfaces and output escaping to prevent untrusted data injections.", "answer_text_with_citations": "Organizations should check the syntax and semantics of system inputs, including character set, length, numerical range, and acceptable values. They should also use parameterized interfaces and output escaping to prevent untrusted data injections.

Citations: si-10_gdn, si-10.6_gdn (NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5)", "evidence_spans": [{"source_id": "si-10_gdn", "span_text": "Checking the valid syntax and semantics of system inputs—including character set, length, numerical range, and acceptable values—verifies that inputs match specified definitions for format and content. For example, if the organization specifies that numerical values between 1-100 are the only acceptable inputs for a field in a given application, inputs of "387," "abc," or "%K%" are invalid inputs and are not accepted as input to the system. Valid inputs are likely to vary from field to field within a software application. Applications typically follow well-defined protocols that use structured messages (i.e., commands or queries) to communicate between software modules or system components. Structured messages can contain raw or unstructured data interspersed with metadata or control information. If software applications use attacker-supplied inputs to construct structured messages without properly encoding such messages, then the attacker could insert malicious commands or special characters that can cause the data to be interpreted as control information or metadata. Consequently, the module or component that receives the corrupted output will perform the wrong operations or otherwise interpret the data incorrectly. Prescreening inputs prior to passing them to interpreters prevents the content from being unintentionally interpreted as commands. Input validation ensures accurate and correct inputs and prevents attacks such as cross-site scripting and a variety of injection attacks."}, {"source_id": "si-10.6_gdn", "span_text": "Untrusted data injections may be prevented using a parameterized interface or output escaping (output encoding). Parameterized interfaces separate data from code so that injections of malicious or unintended data cannot change the semantics of commands being sent. Output escaping uses specified characters to inform the interpreter’s parser whether data is trusted. Prevention of untrusted data injections are with respect to the information inputs defined by the organization in the base control ( [SI-10](#si-10))."}], "status": "OK", "odp_required_list": [], "ask_list": [], "primary_citation": "si-10_gdn", "all_citations": "si-10_gdn, si-10.6_gdn", "contract_mode": "generative_rag_baseline", "debug": {"query_plan": {"original_query": "How should organizations validate system inputs to prevent injection or cross-site scripting attacks?", "rewrites": ["What are the best practices for validating system inputs to avoid injection and cross-site scripting attacks?", "How can organizations ensure the validation of system inputs to mitigate injection and cross-site scripting risks?", "What methods should companies use to validate input data in systems to protect against injection and cross-site scripting vulnerabilities?"]}, "retrieval_meta": {"base_margin_ratio": 0.03922282290811392, "base_top1": "SI-10", "base_top2": "SI-10(6)", "bm25_top1": null, "dense_top1": null, "final_margin_ratio": 0.2592903971672058, "final_top1": "SI-10", "n_docs": 31, "num_variants": 4, "ranked_controls": ["SI-10", "SI-10(6)", "SI-10(3)", "SI-10(5)", "SR-4(3)", "CP-4(2)", "SC-7(10)", "AC-3(9)", "AC-4(20)", "AU-10(4)", "CP-7(1)", "AU-10(2)", "CA-5(1)", "CA-2(3)", "IA-5(8)", "CP-6(1)", "SI-10(1)", "CP-6", "AC-7", "SI-7(12)", "CP-7(4)", "PE-3(2)", "SA-10(3)", "SI-18", "CM-7(5)", "SA-10(1)", "SA-4(3)", "CP-7", "SI-10(4)", "AU-16", "AU-3", "CM-3(2)", "CM-2(2)", "MA-2", "AC-20(1)", "IA-4(6)", "SC-46", "SI-7(17)", "SC-5(3)", "CM-7(1)", "MA-7", "SA-20", "SI-18(3)", "SI-3(6)", "PL-4(1)", "SA-9(7)", "CP-9(5)", "AT-2(4)", "CP-7(3)", "SC-7(21)"], "rerank_alpha": 0.65, "rerank_applied": true, "rerank_apply_min_margin_ratio": 0.15, "rerank_effective_apply_min_margin_ratio": 0.1, "rerank_margin_ratio": 0.2592903971672058, "rerank_skip_min_base_margin_ratio": 0.1, "rerank_skip_require_top1_agreement": false, "rerank_top1": "SI-10", 